IMC PROSPERITY MANUAL CHALLENGE R3:

PART 1: OPTIMAL FIRST BID (b1) - PURE OPTIMIZATION:

Probability that bid b1 exceeds a random reserve:

    P(trade) = (b1 - 670) / 250

Profit per successful trade (buy at b1, sell at 920):

    profit per trade = 920 - b1

Expected profit across N sellers:

    Pi_1(b1) = N * [(b1 - 670) / 250] * (920 - b1)

This is a downward-opening parabola in b1. Maximize by taking the derivative:

    d(Pi_1)/d(b1) = N/250 * [(920 - b1) - (b1 - 670)] = 0

    920 - b1 - b1 + 670 = 0
    2 * b1 = 1590

    b1* = 795

This is simply the MIDPOINT of 670 and 920.

Verification:
    Pi_1(670) = 0       (never trades)
    Pi_1(795) = N * 0.5 * 125 = 62.5N    <-- maximum
    Pi_1(920) = 0       (zero margin)

RESULT: b1 = 795 XIRECs. Always. No assumptions needed.


PART 2: OPTIMAL SECOND BID (b2) - GAME THEORY:

CASE A: b2 >= mu (no penalty)
-------------------------------
Same structure as b1. Profit:

    Pi_2(b2) = N * [(b2 - 670) / 250] * (920 - b2)

Unconstrained optimum is again 795.
If we are confident b2 will be above the average, bid 795.


CASE B: b2 < mu (penalty applies)
-----------------------------------
Substitute the penalty into the profit function:

    Pi_2(b2, mu) = N * [(b2 - 670) / 250] * (920 - b2) * [(920 - mu) / (920 - b2)]^3

The (920 - b2) terms partially cancel:

    Pi_2(b2, mu) = N * (b2 - 670) * (920 - mu)^3
                   ----------------------------------
                        250 * (920 - b2)^2

Since (920 - mu)^3 is a constant, we only need to maximize:

    f(b2) = (b2 - 670) / (920 - b2)^2

Take the derivative:

    f'(b2) = [(920 - b2)^2 + 2*(b2 - 670)*(920 - b2)] / (920 - b2)^4

           = [(920 - b2) + 2*(b2 - 670)] / (920 - b2)^3

Set to zero:

    920 - b2 + 2*b2 - 1340 = 0
    b2 = 420

420 is OUTSIDE the valid range [670, 920] 

This means the penalized profit function is STRICTLY INCREASING throughout
the entire valid range [670, 920]. There is no interior maximum.


PENALTY TABLE (assuming mu = 810)
----------------------------------

    b2      S factor                    Effective multiplier
    ------  --------------------------  --------------------
    920     1.000                       100%  (but zero margin)
    850     1.000                       100%  (above mu)
    810     1.000                       100%  (at mu, boundary)
    795     (110/125)^3  = 0.681        68%
    760     (110/160)^3  = 0.299        30%
    720     (110/200)^3  = 0.166        17%

The cubic exponent makes being below the average EXTREMELY costly.
A bid of 760 (only 50 XIRECs below mu=810) already wipes out 70% of profit.


GAME-THEORETIC EQUILIBRIUM
---------------------------
Each team's best response is to bid at or above mu. This creates upward pressure:

    1. Rational teams recognize they should bid >= mu
    2. This raises mu
    3. Which pushes everyone higher

The cap at b2 = 920 (zero margin) stops the loop.

In practice, based on historical IMC round data, most teams cluster near
the naive optimum of 795. The empirical average ends up around 795-820.

A moderate upward shade of ~5-25 XIRECs above 795 provides strong insurance
against the penalty without significantly sacrificing margin.


STRATEGY GUIDE
--------------

    Our belief about mu         Recommended b2    Reasoning
    -------------------------   ---------------   ----------------------------------
    Teams bid naively, mu~795   795               No risk, maximum margin
    Some shading, mu~810-830    820-830           Stay above mean, small sacrifice
    Most teams aware, mu>850    Match mu est.     Never be below average


================================================================================
FINAL ANSWERS
================================================================================

    b1* = 795 XIRECs        (always -- analytically exact)

    b2* = max(795, mu_estimate), rounded to nearest 5

where mu_estimate is our best guess of the average second bid across all teams.

If uncertain: we submit b2 = 795. Empirically, most teams do not shade aggressively,
so 795 is usually at or above the actual average.


